138:"**The answers this rung follows.** Each repair is the checker probe's, measured (explorations/perf-probes/checker-bound-defects/REPORT.md, sections 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 and 7): write your tests first, then apply its three patches, read them and own them. Rows 686, 687, 689 and 690 have one cause, traced (section 2.2): TypeAnalyzer.normConjunct expands a closed trait over a conjunct that names a type or inference variable, and the union rule, first-match in the order of the type group's subtyping figure (Papers/Types/fig-subtype.tick), then splits the union before the variable's bound is read. One change there clears all four; a guard in the solver does not (section 2.3). The change keeps every union the checker builds free of variables, the condition under which the POPL 2019 paper proves matching complete (research/extracts/ParkPOPL2019-extract.md section 6), the type group's late word (P-26). The sibling patch is in this rung by the coordinator's call, listed for review: it fixes a crash of the same root at a ground second bound, was measured green on the three tracks, and keeps the team's expansion for ground meets (section 2.5); its solver takes the intersection of the upper bounds, as P-48 says. Row 680 is the checker's, not plane's body: the local function group's return-type thunks are primed without the group in scope (impls/Decls.scala:414), while the specification makes the local functions of one scope mutually recursive (Specification/basic/functions.tex, \"Local Function Declarations\"); the library line is valid as written (section 4). No decision of the curator covers the analyzer's normal form (section 10).",
