207:"- Row 707, no distance site: checkOverloading counts a duplicate only where oracle.equiv holds and the self positions are equal (ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/typechecker/OverloadingChecker.scala:441, the team's line). So f[\\T extends S\\](self, y: T) beside f[\\U extends S\\](x: U, self), and the plain pair f(self, y: S) beside f(x: S, self), pass it, and the code generator stops: 'Likely bug in static analysis, apparently malformed overload set not rejected' (ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/OverloadSet.java:409-411). The repair: two functional methods, each with a self position, are a duplicate where oracle.equiv holds, wherever their self parameters stand; a top-level function beside a functional method keeps its own check (:405-410). The message prints a functional method's domain without self: say which of the pair's it prints. Walk refuses both pairs at load. The library declares one such pair, by this record's review's grep of Library/ and ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/: object FlatString's opr ||(self, b:FlatString) and opr ||(a:FlatString, self) (Library/FlatString.fsi:17, :20; the second is row 585's api line, which the component lacks, PLAN item 38); String's ||, |||, // and /// pair (self, b:Any) with (a:Any, self), which are not equivalent. The checker never reaches FlatString's pair, since it checks no overloading of a symbolic operator (isDeclaredName, OverloadingChecker.scala:720-724; row 584), and your repair leaves that as it is: an error in an api stops the count stage before it checks any component (ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/phases/TypeCheckPhase.java:45-46), so a refused FlatString api would hide the component's count. Grep again and report each pair, with whether your check reaches it.",
